+# Palbase Android SDK
+
+Native Kotlin runtime and typed backend code generation for Palbase applications.
+The aggregate artifact is `io.palbase:palbe`; the Gradle plugin is
+`io.palbase.codegen`.
+
+- Android minSdk 26, compileSdk 36
+- Kotlin 2.2, coroutines, StateFlow, kotlinx.serialization
+- Cancellable OkHttp transport with GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, and QUERY
+- Encrypted session persistence backed by AndroidKeyStore
+- OpenAPI-driven, deterministic `pb.<namespace>.<operation>` generation
+- Closed runtime AARs; codegen source and generated API documentation artifacts
+
+## Getting the artifacts
+
+The SDK is closed-source and ships as binaries from a public Maven repository,
+[palgroup/palbackend-android](https://github.com/palgroup/palbackend-android) —
+the Android twin of `palbackend-ios`. No account, no token. `settings.gradle.kts`
+declares it twice: once for the codegen plugin (its marker's group is
+`io.palbase.codegen`, not `io.palbase`, so the plugin side matches by prefix) and
+once for the libraries:
+
+```kotlin
+pluginManagement {
+    repositories {
+        google()
+        maven {
+            url = uri("https://palgroup.github.io/palbackend-android/")
+            content { includeGroupByRegex("io\\.palbase.*") }
+        }
+        mavenCentral()
+        gradlePluginPortal()
+    }
+}
+
+dependencyResolutionManagement {
+    repositories {
+        google()
+        mavenCentral()
+        maven {
+            url = uri("https://palgroup.github.io/palbackend-android/")
+            content { includeGroup("io.palbase") }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+The `content {}` filters keep Gradle from asking this repository about every
+other dependency. The same artifacts are also on GitHub Packages
+(`maven.pkg.github.com/palgroup/palbackend-android-src`), which needs a
+`read:packages` token even to read; the public repository is the documented path.
+
+What the SDK needs from the app:
+
+| Requirement | Value |
+|---|---|
+| `minSdk` | 26 |
+| `compileSdk` | 36 — the AARs are compiled against it |
+| Kotlin | 2.2.x — the SDK is built with 2.2.21, and 2.2.21 consumers are what is tested |
+| Plugin | `org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization` on the module that hosts the generated client |
+| JVM target | 17 |
+| Firebase | the SDK pulls Firebase BOM 34 for push; Gradle picks that BOM, and 34 no longer ships the `-ktx` artifacts. Depend on `firebase-messaging`, `firebase-crashlytics`, `firebase-config`, not their `-ktx` twins |
+| Manifest | `palbe-notifications` declares `com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_channel_id` (`palbase_default`). An app that sets the same `<meta-data>` adds `tools:replace="android:value"` to its own, or the merger stops the build |
+
+The library and the plugin must be the SAME version: the plugin generates code
+against that version's runtime, and the two are released together (see
+[Release](#release)).
+
+## Consumer setup
+
+The CLI owns native app linkage and the generated config slots:
+
+```bash
+palbase link <target>
+# Subsequent config or backend updates:
+palbase link
+```
+
+Apply the plugin and aggregate SDK in the Android app:
+
+```kotlin
+plugins {
+    id("com.android.application")
+    id("org.jetbrains.kotlin.android")
+    id("org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization")
+    id("io.palbase.codegen") version "<palbase-version>"
+}
+
+dependencies {
+    implementation("io.palbase:palbe:<palbase-version>")
+}
+```
+
+The plugin reads ONE environment out of `palbase/environments/<environment>/`,
+which is where `palbase link` writes that environment's `openapi.json` — which
+carries the role definitions inside itself, as `x-palbase-roles` — and its
+`android-config.json`. Which environment a build compiles is
+the `palbase.env` Gradle property, and `local` when it is unset:
+
+```
+./gradlew assembleDebug -Ppalbase.env=main
+```
+
+Set it in `gradle.properties` to change the default for a checkout, or pass it
+per build; a CI job passes the environment it releases. A selected environment
+with no directory FAILS the build naming itself — nothing is ever generated from
+an environment that was not asked for.
+
+The plugin generates Kotlin sources per Android variant and packages the runtime
+config into the APK as `palbase/palbase-config.json`; no generated Kotlin needs
+to be committed. The app config must contain nonempty `app_id`, `base_url`, and
+`api_key` values written by the CLI.
+
+Point the plugin somewhere else when `palbase/` lives beside the app module
+rather than inside it:
+
+```kotlin
+palbase {
+    environmentsDir.set(rootProject.layout.projectDirectory.dir("palbase/environments"))
+}
+```
+
+Initialize once in `Application.onCreate`:
+
+```kotlin
+class App : Application() {
+    override fun onCreate() {
+        super.onCreate()
+        Palbase.initialize(this)
+    }
+}
+```
+
+Use auth, generated contracts, or the untyped escape hatch through `pb`:
+
+```kotlin
+val signedIn = pb.auth.signIn("user@example.com", "password")
+pb.auth.state.collect { state -> /* SignedIn or SignedOut */ }
+
+val created: RoomsCreateResponse =
+    pb.rooms.create(RoomsCreateRequest(name = "Android"))
+
+@Serializable data class CreateTodo(val title: String)
+@Serializable data class Todo(val id: String, val title: String)
+val todo: Todo = pb.call("todos", CreateTodo("buy milk"))
+```
+
+## OAuth
+
+Configure clients per application, platform and variant in Studio or through
+`palbase auth providers config set`. `palbase link` picks one of those
+configurations and writes its public `oauth` snapshot into the environment's
+`android-config.json` — that snapshot is the ONLY thing the Gradle plugin reads
+about OAuth. Which configuration gets snapshotted is a `palbase link` question,
+answered by `oauth.android` in the CLI's own committed project file
+(`application_key` and `variant`, plus `client_keys` when several clients of a
+provider match); flavors and application ID suffixes require explicit selection.
+The plugin checks the snapshot against the final variant's application ID, and
+the runtime checks Google's signing certificate against the installed APK.
+
+```kotlin
+val result = pb.auth.signInWithGoogle(activity)
+when (result) {
+    is OAuthResult.SignedIn -> showHome(result.success.user)
+    is OAuthResult.MfaRequired -> showMfa(result.token, result.factors)
+    is OAuthResult.Linked -> showLinked(result.identity)
+}
+```
+
+Google Credential Manager receives the **Web application** `server_client_id`
+and the nonce returned by the backend transaction. The separate Android client
+ID belongs to the package and SHA-1 certificate; it is never substituted for the
+server ID. Apple on Android uses a browser client with its Services ID. Microsoft
+can use a native PKCE client with its configured directory and redirect URI;
+GitHub uses browser OAuth.
+
+```kotlin
+// Observe completion before opening the browser (for example in a lifecycle scope).
+pb.auth.linkResults.collect { event ->
+    when (event) {
+        is AuthLinkResult.OAuthCompleted -> handleOAuth(event.result)
+        is AuthLinkResult.Failed -> showError(event.error)
+        else -> Unit
+    }
+}
+// From the sign-in button's coroutine:
+pb.auth.openOAuth(activity, OAuthProvider.Apple)
+```
+
+The plugin emits the selected clients' exact callback intent filters. Keep the
+configured return URI unchanged. `beginOAuth` and `completeOAuth` allow custom
+presentation; `pendingOAuth`, `resumeOAuth(transactionId)` and `cancelOAuth`
+support recovery after recreation. Completion proof and payload are stored
+under Android Keystore before provider UI or completion HTTP. A transient failure
+retries the same payload; another result cannot replace it. There is no in-memory
+fallback if secure storage fails.
+
+Use `OAuthPurpose.Link` while already signed in to link an identity. Linking
+requires a recent verified sign-in, never matches accounts by email, and does
+not create another session. MFA also requires completion before there is a
+signed-in session. Cancellation is `AuthError.Cancelled`.
+
+## Verification
+
+Use Android Studio's bundled JDK 21 locally:
+
+```bash
+export JAVA_HOME="/Applications/Android Studio.app/Contents/jbr/Contents/Home"
+export ANDROID_HOME="$HOME/Library/Android/sdk"
+
+# Unit, ABI, lint, generated-source, and minified consumer gates
+./gradlew check lintRelease :consumer-release:assembleRelease \
+  --configuration-cache --no-daemon
+
+# Gradle TestKit plugin suite
+./gradlew -p codegen-gradle check --configuration-cache --no-daemon
+
+# Maven shape, source-leak, version alignment, and optional-ABI isolation
+./scripts/verify-publications.sh
+
+# AndroidKeyStore on a managed API 26 device; live test skips without credentials
+./gradlew :palbe-core:pixelApi26DebugAndroidTest --no-daemon
+
+# Real backend smoke on an attached device or running emulator
+./gradlew :palbe-core:connectedDebugAndroidTest \
+  -Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.palbaseApiKey=pb_<ref>_c... \
+  -Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.palbaseBaseUrl=https://<ref>.dev.palbase.studio \
+  -Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.palbaseEmail=<test-user> \
+  -Pandroid.testInstrumentationRunnerArguments.palbasePassword=<test-password>
+```
+
+The `palbe-core/api/palbe-core.api` golden file is checked by `check`. Update it
+with `./gradlew :palbe-core:apiDump` only after deliberately reviewing public API
+changes.
+
+## Debug tracing (opt-in)
+
+The transport logs every request and response to logcat under the tag
+`io.palbase.sdk`, with secrets redacted. It is **off by default** and cannot
+ship enabled: the switch is the device's own log property, set per run:
+
+```bash
+adb shell setprop log.tag.io.palbase.sdk DEBUG
+adb logcat -s io.palbase.sdk
+```
+
+Each request logs its method, URL, headers (credentials masked) and body; each
+response logs status, duration and the server's request id, plus the error body
+when the status is not 2xx. The in-app console (`pb.debug`) is a separate,
+always-on recorder with its own storage; this switch is the developer's own tap.
+
+## Release
+
+Artefacts are published by hand, and the codegen plugin lives in its own build
+under `codegen-gradle/`, so a publish run from the root never reaches it — which
+is how 2.1.0 and 2.2.0 shipped a runtime without a plugin that could compile
+against it. `scripts/publish.sh` publishes everything a consumer resolves in one
+run and refuses to finish until each coordinate, plugin marker included, lists
+the version on GitHub Packages:
+
+```bash
+git tag v2.3.0
+PALBE_VERSION=2.3.0 scripts/publish.sh
+git push origin main v2.3.0
+```
+
+It requires a clean tree, HEAD tagged with that version, JDK 21 in `JAVA_HOME`,
+and a credential with `write:packages`.
